      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Method"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Make the ragu: cook the onion, carrot, and celery in a little olive oil for 8 minutes over medium heat. Add the minced lamb and brown for 5 minutes. Pour in the red wine and let it evaporate. Add the tinned tomatoes and cook uncovered over low heat for 45 minutes until thick. Cool completely."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Make the saffron risotto: heat a little olive oil and butter in a large saucepan. Add the remaining onion and cook for 5 minutes. Add the rice and toast for 2 minutes. Add the saffron water and begin adding warm stock a ladleful at a time, stirring continuously, for 18 to 20 minutes. Stir in the parmesan and remaining butter. Spread onto a tray and cool completely, at least 1 hour."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "To shape: wet your hands. Take about 80 g of cold risotto and flatten into a disc. Place a teaspoon of ragu and a cube of mozzarella in the centre. Cup your hands around the filling and press the rice firmly around it to form a ball. Repeat with all the rice."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Set up three bowls: flour, beaten egg, and breadcrumbs. Roll each arancino in flour, then egg, then breadcrumbs. Press the crumbs on firmly."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Heat the oil to 175C in a deep saucepan. Fry the arancini in batches of 3 or 4 for 4 to 5 minutes, turning, until deep golden all over. Drain on kitchen paper and serve hot."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "troubleshooter",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "issue": "Arancini falling apart in the oil"
            },
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "The risotto was not cold enough or the crumbing was incomplete. Chill the shaped arancini for 20 minutes in the fridge before frying if they feel soft."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "issue": "Pale or greasy coating"
            },
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "The oil temperature dropped. Do not overcrowd the pan and maintain 175C throughout. Use a thermometer."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "issue": "Filling leaking out"
            },
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Too much filling or the rice was not pressed firmly enough around it. Use a smaller amount of filling and press the rice very firmly to seal."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where this dish lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Arancini are the street food of Sicily. They are sold from market stalls and food carts across the island. Palermo and Catania each claim the definitive version. In Catania they are shaped into cones; in Palermo they are round. Both are delicious. They are best eaten hot, straight from the oil."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
